WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.240
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:06.404
BRIAN GRANT: Hi.

00:00:06.404 --> 00:00:07.070
I'm Brian Grant.

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:08.630
I'm a principal
engineer at Google.

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:12.140
And I'm here today with
Aparna Sinha, product manager

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:14.810
on Kubernetes and
Container Engine,

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:17.360
and Brandon Philips, CTO CoreOS.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:20.236
And I'm going to talk to you
about how and why we built

00:00:20.236 --> 00:00:22.610
the Kubernetes community,
which is one of the things that

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:25.370
really sets Kubernetes
apart from the other content

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:30.302
orchestration projects and
products that are out there.

00:00:30.302 --> 00:00:32.000
So application
containers-- you've

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.270
been hearing a lot
about the conference.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:34.728
They're an increasingly
popular way

00:00:34.728 --> 00:00:37.940
to accelerate application
development and deployment.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:40.190
But containers alone
often aren't sufficient

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:43.552
for managing containers
and production at scale.

00:00:43.552 --> 00:00:45.260
You need a container
orchestration system

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:48.410
like Kubernetes.

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:52.550
I like to think of Kubernetes
as a container-centric

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:58.650
infrastructure for managing
and deploying applications.

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:02.261
Kubernetes was
created by Google,

00:01:02.261 --> 00:01:04.260
drawing on experience
with Borg, the system that

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:07.710
runs Search and
G-Mail, but Kubernetes

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:11.040
was designed to run anywhere.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:12.840
I previously worked
on Borg and have

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.050
been involved with Kubernetes
since its inception.

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:17.639
We open-sourced
Kubernetes in 2014

00:01:17.639 --> 00:01:19.680
and created the Cloud
Native Computing Foundation

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.930
to host the Kubernetes
project, and also

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:28.350
to foster an ecosystem of
cloud native infrastructure.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:31.200
As you can see from this
graph of commits over time,

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.080
Kubernetes has been
growing very fast.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:38.637
We sustained a
steady pace of growth

00:01:38.637 --> 00:01:40.470
in the original Kubernetes
repository, which

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:41.670
is what this graph shows--

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:44.130
now with more than
43,000 commits.

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:45.960
But changes to this
original repository

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:49.350
account for fewer than half
of all changes to the project.

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:52.050
So it really is
growing very fast.

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:54.690
It's been estimated that
over 400 years of work

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:59.030
have been put into Kubernetes
in just the last three years.

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:03.920
This growth has been fueled
by growth of contributors.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:06.350
We started with
fewer than a dozen

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:09.680
and now we have around 150
active monthly contributors

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:14.330
to the project, and over 1,500
all-time unique contributors.

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:16.350
We have contributors
in most time zones,

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:19.110
including countries like
Brazil, India, China,

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:20.400
and all over the world.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:23.750
In fact, anywhere you
go, you're likely to find

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:29.274
an active Kubernetes
contributor community.

00:02:29.274 --> 00:02:30.940
So where does all
this growth come from?

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:34.441
Google has been increasing
its investment in Kubernetes

00:02:34.441 --> 00:02:35.440
over the past few years.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:37.180
We've been doubling
in size every year.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:39.440
But the community has
grown even faster.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:42.730
So in the first year, Google
accounted for about 3/4

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:45.160
of all the changes
to Kubernetes.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:47.280
Now, we're responsible
for less than half.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:49.240
We worked actually
really, really hard

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:51.819
to onboard all these new
contributors onto the project,

00:02:51.819 --> 00:02:53.860
and Google engineers spend
a substantial fraction

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:57.520
of their time helping other
contributors on the product

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:01.317
onboard and be successful.

00:03:01.317 --> 00:03:03.400
So we put all this work
into growing the community

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:05.620
because we found that
engagement with the community

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:08.000
yields numerous benefits.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:12.070
Engagements with users helps
those users be successful

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:14.530
with the project,
and it also provides

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:18.700
rapid feedback that drives
improvements to Kubernetes.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:23.030
And both the improvements
and the direct help to users

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:25.600
create advocates for the
project to blog and tweet

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:27.580
about it, who
present at meet-ups

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:29.530
and grow awareness
of the product, which

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:31.420
then attracts more users.

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:36.130
And more users and more usage
also attracts developers,

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:40.270
and the developers build
tools and other projects

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:42.730
that help make Kubernetes
easier to use--

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:49.540
things like package
managers, chat bots,

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:51.640
platform as a service,
functions as service,

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:53.620
event driven automation.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:57.790
I have a document six
pages long with just a list

00:03:57.790 --> 00:04:00.550
of interesting projects
built on Kubernetes.

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:03.100
And we see that this empowerment
to improve the project

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:07.420
increases their commitment and
spurs innovation of even more

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:09.890
projects based on Kubernetes.

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:12.190
These users and
developers additionally

00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:14.440
attract vendors, who try
to fill the remaining

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:15.990
gaps in the ecosystem.

00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:19.279
And we help these vendors
integrate with Kubernetes

00:04:19.279 --> 00:04:21.449
and that enables Kubernetes
in more environments

00:04:21.449 --> 00:04:22.240
and for more users.

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:27.970
So all of this creates
this virtuous cycle

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:30.880
that has made Kubernetes one
of the most popular projects

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:36.040
on GitHub with over
20,000 [INAUDIBLE] stars.

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:38.490
But we like to say Kubernetes
isn't just open source--

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:40.220
it's an open community.

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:42.350
The open community
encourages not only

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:46.400
direct contributions but
also sharing best practices,

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:47.315
examples, tools.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:53.420
And we find that not only
vendors and developers

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:55.790
are contributing,
but also our users.

00:04:55.790 --> 00:04:58.220
For example, a
co-founder of Box,

00:04:58.220 --> 00:05:01.134
who is using Kubernetes
in production on premise,

00:05:01.134 --> 00:05:03.300
actually wrote the initial
version of the Kubernetes

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:04.975
command line tool.

00:05:04.975 --> 00:05:07.100
And people may contribute
for personal or corporate

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:10.730
recognition, or they may
contribute to Kubernetes

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:13.250
to make it better suit their
needs just because it's

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:17.170
cheaper than building their
own solution from scratch.

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:18.800
Or they may do it
for the greater good,

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:22.000
because making Kubernetes better
makes it better for everyone

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:23.910
and benefits everyone,
not just Google.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:28.750
In fact, our community
is often cited

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:31.930
by users who talk about
their experience as one

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:34.450
of the main reasons
they chose Kubernetes

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:35.805
over the alternatives.

00:05:35.805 --> 00:05:37.930
People say that they want
to be part of a community

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:39.640
where other members
of the community

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:42.040
give back to the
community as well.

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:54.000
So there are costs and
risks associated with having

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:54.960
such an open community.

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:57.000
It's not all the
positive, so I did

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:00.150
want to cover the challenges.

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:03.600
First, it's extremely
time-consuming.

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:08.070
We have a lot of interaction
with users, developers,

00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:11.880
vendors, everybody in
lots of different forums--

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:16.260
Twitter, Slack, GitHub, email
lists, Meetups, conferences,

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:18.360
interviews.

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:21.780
So that engagement
provides all the benefits

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:25.050
I just talked about, but
it is very time-consuming.

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:27.780
And it's not just
a nine-to-five job.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:31.410
A lot of people contribute to
Kubernetes in the evenings,

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:35.080
on the weekend-- they do it for
fun, not part of their day job.

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:37.080
And like I said before,
they do it in time zones

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:37.871
all over the world.

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:44.840
We found that while GitHub
is really good for building

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:47.520
a community of open
source contributors,

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:49.340
it makes it really
challenging to manage

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:53.180
a large project with large
teams and many different moving

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:55.340
pieces.

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:58.250
Decision making, while it
can lead to better decisions

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:02.930
because you get a
diverse set of inputs,

00:07:02.930 --> 00:07:08.330
it also does slow down
decision making to some degree.

00:07:08.330 --> 00:07:11.810
So you have to be aware of that.

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:15.860
And finally, effort is not
allocated on the project

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:19.540
like it would be if it were just
executed by a single company.

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:22.010
A single company
can control how much

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:26.060
work is put into features versus
code quality, for example,

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:27.440
or documentation.

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:33.020
And with people contributing
under their own objectives

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:37.310
and not under your
control, steering people

00:07:37.310 --> 00:07:42.780
to meet all the needs of
the project is challenging.

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:44.670
So now, I'm going to
hand off to Aparna,

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:48.000
who's going to talk to you about
some of the processes of how

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.870
the project actually works.

00:07:49.870 --> 00:07:52.170
APARNA SINHA: Thank you, Brian.

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:54.580
So Brian mentioned
some of our challenges,

00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:57.190
and we are actively
working on challenges,

00:07:57.190 --> 00:08:00.080
and they are always upcoming.

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:02.320
We're only three
years old as far

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:04.160
as open source projects goes.

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:07.045
Kubernetes is still
kind of a toddler.

00:08:07.045 --> 00:08:08.420
There are certain
things, though,

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:12.520
that we have learned in our
three years of reasonably

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:16.570
well success as far as number
of contributors and number

00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:18.010
of contributions.

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:20.170
Here are the principles
that we've learned.

00:08:20.170 --> 00:08:23.790
The number one principle
is that of inclusion.

00:08:23.790 --> 00:08:27.430
And by inclusion, we mean that
we are a welcoming community,

00:08:27.430 --> 00:08:32.020
and we value and respect
diversity of opinion.

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:35.770
This is easier said than done.

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:39.110
We have a lot of very senior
engineers on this project--

00:08:39.110 --> 00:08:41.840
a lot of them from Google
and from other companies.

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:44.290
And it can be
difficult to disagree.

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:47.170
It can be difficult to be
open-minded and include

00:08:47.170 --> 00:08:51.460
other opinions and reconcile
those, especially when there

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:52.840
is time pressure.

00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:57.130
We have a release cadence, where
we release every three months.

00:08:57.130 --> 00:09:00.050
And there is time pressure
to make those decisions.

00:09:00.050 --> 00:09:02.050
So in those circumstances,
we've actually

00:09:02.050 --> 00:09:04.660
found it's even more
important to be inclusive

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:05.740
and to be respectful.

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:08.200
And we've got a code
of conduct around that.

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:09.580
And we actually benefit--

00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:12.970
particularly Google--
benefits from this principle

00:09:12.970 --> 00:09:16.030
of inclusion because it takes
us outside of our sandbox

00:09:16.030 --> 00:09:17.980
and our limited
view and gives us

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:20.830
a much better view
of the full set

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:24.790
of users, both within Google
and then outside of Google,

00:09:24.790 --> 00:09:27.800
that will be using
this as a platform.

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:34.460
So that's the number one
principle, is inclusion.

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:38.040
Another aspect of
inclusion is that we

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:40.740
have this process called--

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:44.310
this construct,
organizational construct--

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:46.560
called special interest groups.

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:50.880
When a project gets to
be large like Kubernetes,

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:53.940
it is important to push
the decision making down

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:58.050
to the actual people who
are building the features.

00:09:58.050 --> 00:10:00.750
And that's what the
SIGs allow us to do.

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:03.120
Each of the SIGs
is a combination

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:05.710
of people from
different companies

00:10:05.710 --> 00:10:08.460
and different organizations
that have gotten together

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:10.770
because they're interested
in a particular topic--

00:10:10.770 --> 00:10:12.400
a particular technical topic--

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:16.530
and they want to move the needle
in Kubernetes on that topic.

00:10:16.530 --> 00:10:19.050
These are two examples.

00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:21.680
There's a SIG called
Cluster Lifecycle SIG.

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:24.140
And their main task
there is to make sure

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:27.350
that Kubernetes is easy
to install and deploy

00:10:27.350 --> 00:10:29.480
in a variety of
different environments.

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:31.010
This is a hard problem.

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:33.230
Kubernetes has
become very popular

00:10:33.230 --> 00:10:37.610
and is fundamentally a
portable cluster orchestrator.

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:39.740
That means that you can
deploy it on Bare Metal.

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:43.440
You can deploy it on any of
the various public clouds,

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:46.340
all of which have slightly
different networking, slightly

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:51.620
different load balancing, and
slightly different storage.

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:54.560
So it needs to be able
to be installed easily

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:58.610
on a variety of
different environments.

00:10:58.610 --> 00:11:01.490
In fact, last year we got
some feedback from our users

00:11:01.490 --> 00:11:04.970
that Kubernetes is becoming
hard to install and hard to use,

00:11:04.970 --> 00:11:07.930
and that day one
experience isn't very good.

00:11:07.930 --> 00:11:09.436
SIG Cluster
Lifecycle, which, you

00:11:09.436 --> 00:11:11.560
see some of the companies
that are involved in it--

00:11:11.560 --> 00:11:16.960
Weaveworks, CoreOS, Heptio,
obviously Google, and others.

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:19.930
SIG Cluster Lifecycle
kicked into high gear,

00:11:19.930 --> 00:11:23.500
and within one release they
came out with a much simpler

00:11:23.500 --> 00:11:26.540
two-command install experience--

00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:32.752
a command line experience that
is labeled Cube ADM. And then

00:11:32.752 --> 00:11:34.210
over the last two
quarters, they've

00:11:34.210 --> 00:11:36.190
built out a roadmap
that is resonating

00:11:36.190 --> 00:11:39.335
much more with users.

00:11:39.335 --> 00:11:41.710
What they're doing is they're
taking some of the building

00:11:41.710 --> 00:11:44.170
blocks from Cube
ADM and making them

00:11:44.170 --> 00:11:47.320
reusable across all
the different methods

00:11:47.320 --> 00:11:50.110
of installation,
bringing consistency

00:11:50.110 --> 00:11:53.110
and ease of use across
different environments.

00:11:53.110 --> 00:11:55.750
This type of hard problem--

00:11:55.750 --> 00:11:57.520
it's hard for one
company to solve it

00:11:57.520 --> 00:11:59.980
because you don't
necessarily have a view

00:11:59.980 --> 00:12:02.540
across the different platforms.

00:12:02.540 --> 00:12:06.100
But when multiple interested
parties get together,

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:08.680
they have a better view of
the problem and a better shot

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:11.950
at solving this type of issue.

00:12:11.950 --> 00:12:14.860
Another example is
SIG Service Catalog.

00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:19.060
This is also a collaboration
between different companies.

00:12:19.060 --> 00:12:21.520
They got together
and they worked

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:25.810
on a common standard for service
to service communication,

00:12:25.810 --> 00:12:30.210
as well as for services to
be consumed in Kubernetes.

00:12:30.210 --> 00:12:32.760
They borrowed and
worked collaboratively

00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:35.940
with Cloud Foundry, which is
another open source project,

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:39.450
and brought the two
projects closer together.

00:12:39.450 --> 00:12:43.710
And jointly, we're working to
establish the Open Services

00:12:43.710 --> 00:12:44.922
Broker API.

00:12:44.922 --> 00:12:46.880
This was announced, I
think, earlier this year.

00:12:49.440 --> 00:12:52.670
That's a great example of
skating to where the puck is.

00:12:52.670 --> 00:12:56.310
This SIG is enabling
service management

00:12:56.310 --> 00:13:01.474
in a way that will be used
at scale in the future.

00:13:01.474 --> 00:13:02.640
Those are just two examples.

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:05.430
We have over 25 special
interest groups,

00:13:05.430 --> 00:13:06.870
and you can see
some of the areas

00:13:06.870 --> 00:13:10.080
that the community is
particularly passionate about.

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:12.600
And each of these
is an active group,

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:15.040
and these SIGs are
always evolving.

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:17.429
So it's kind of like
different stars--

00:13:17.429 --> 00:13:19.470
they're colliding and
collapsing and the new ones

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:24.240
are being born as the interest
and the problems of our users

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:25.080
change.

00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:26.260
That's a very good sign.

00:13:26.260 --> 00:13:31.030
That's a very healthy
sign of the community.

00:13:31.030 --> 00:13:32.640
One of the challenges
when you have

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:34.770
this type of distributed
leadership and distributed

00:13:34.770 --> 00:13:36.060
decision making--

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:38.340
while that helps
you scale, it is

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:41.837
difficult to keep them all
informed and coordinated.

00:13:41.837 --> 00:13:43.920
And so that's one of our
challenges in Kubernetes,

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:47.520
is how do we make sure that
the SIGs are aware of what

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:49.380
the other SIGs are doing?

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:51.660
This comes to a head
when we do a release.

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:55.230
So we've had cases
where one SIG developed

00:13:55.230 --> 00:13:58.650
a feature and another SIG wasn't
aware of what was going on

00:13:58.650 --> 00:14:00.360
and then after the
release, we found

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:05.050
that there was a bug that then
we needed to do a patch on.

00:14:05.050 --> 00:14:07.620
So this is what brings me to
our second principle, which

00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:09.870
is that of transparency.

00:14:09.870 --> 00:14:12.450
Transparency is extremely
important in a project

00:14:12.450 --> 00:14:13.560
of this size.

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:16.660
What that means to
us is that we really

00:14:16.660 --> 00:14:22.050
overtrain on being open
with everything that we do,

00:14:22.050 --> 00:14:26.359
especially all of our
technical content.

00:14:26.359 --> 00:14:27.900
There's only really
one place for it,

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:30.540
which is GitHub Issues
and GitHub Proposals.

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:33.930
It's all developed in the
open-- the designs are,

00:14:33.930 --> 00:14:35.550
the problems are.

00:14:35.550 --> 00:14:38.340
There's a lot of back and
forth between the engineers

00:14:38.340 --> 00:14:41.040
and between different
companies on GitHub.

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:44.320
By some measure, Kubernetes
is one of the most talkative

00:14:44.320 --> 00:14:46.230
communities.

00:14:46.230 --> 00:14:48.870
I think the measure is actually
a number of comments per issue.

00:14:48.870 --> 00:14:53.160
We have a lot of
comments on every issue.

00:14:53.160 --> 00:14:59.430
So that makes it great
in terms of transparency.

00:14:59.430 --> 00:15:02.280
Also, all of our
discussions and meetings

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:06.559
are recorded and
available to the public.

00:15:06.559 --> 00:15:08.100
But the community
is not the only one

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:09.180
who benefits from this.

00:15:09.180 --> 00:15:12.690
Actually at the end of the day,
the users benefit the most.

00:15:12.690 --> 00:15:15.570
And I think that's one of
the attractions of Kubernetes

00:15:15.570 --> 00:15:17.790
and of open source projects
in general that have

00:15:17.790 --> 00:15:19.680
this level of transparency--

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:22.450
is that what you
see is what you get.

00:15:22.450 --> 00:15:25.274
So the code that's in the
open and the discussions that

00:15:25.274 --> 00:15:27.190
are in the open-- that's
exactly what you get.

00:15:27.190 --> 00:15:28.660
There isn't a lot of marketing.

00:15:28.660 --> 00:15:31.870
There isn't a lot
of positioning,

00:15:31.870 --> 00:15:33.910
and users actually
really appreciate that.

00:15:36.990 --> 00:15:39.630
So with the emphasis
on transparency

00:15:39.630 --> 00:15:41.940
comes a large volume
of information.

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:44.490
As I mentioned, we
are a very comment-

00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:46.860
and discussion-oriented
community,

00:15:46.860 --> 00:15:49.630
so it's difficult to actually
manage that information

00:15:49.630 --> 00:15:51.057
overflow.

00:15:51.057 --> 00:15:52.140
And Brian alluded to this.

00:15:52.140 --> 00:15:57.600
It's a 24/7 global kind of job
for everyone who's involved.

00:15:57.600 --> 00:16:00.630
Some of the constructs that
we have for organizing this

00:16:00.630 --> 00:16:01.800
are these meetings.

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:04.350
We have a weekly
community meeting.

00:16:04.350 --> 00:16:06.750
Sarah Novotny, who was in
the keynote this morning--

00:16:06.750 --> 00:16:10.050
she leads that meeting,
and she kicks it off

00:16:10.050 --> 00:16:14.340
by welcoming everybody,
and then there's a demo.

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:15.960
So every meeting
there's a demo, where

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:20.520
the latest and greatest feature
from one of the community

00:16:20.520 --> 00:16:25.210
members gets demoed and others
have a chance to give feedback.

00:16:25.210 --> 00:16:27.474
But it's also really
exciting because usually

00:16:27.474 --> 00:16:29.640
in these community meetings--
which are, by the way,

00:16:29.640 --> 00:16:30.480
open to the public.

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:32.290
You are all welcome
to come to these.

00:16:32.290 --> 00:16:35.290
You'll see the
latest innovation,

00:16:35.290 --> 00:16:37.920
and that's really
exciting actually.

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:40.350
After that, each of the
SIGs give an update.

00:16:40.350 --> 00:16:42.300
And that's one of
the ways that we

00:16:42.300 --> 00:16:44.280
ensure this SIG-to-SIG
communication

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:46.470
so that we don't have those
kinds of issues, where

00:16:46.470 --> 00:16:48.550
one SIG developed a
feature and the other SIG

00:16:48.550 --> 00:16:49.650
didn't know about it.

00:16:49.650 --> 00:16:52.290
Each of the SIGs gives an update
about what they're working on

00:16:52.290 --> 00:16:55.680
for the release, and then
if there are dependencies

00:16:55.680 --> 00:16:58.930
that are discussed, those
can be taken offline

00:16:58.930 --> 00:17:01.210
and the SIGs can work together.

00:17:01.210 --> 00:17:03.870
It's not perfect,
but it's better

00:17:03.870 --> 00:17:06.150
and we're evolving every day.

00:17:06.150 --> 00:17:07.940
Then we also have the
burndown meetings.

00:17:07.940 --> 00:17:10.170
The burndown meetings
are release-specific,

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:14.640
so we are right now just about
to put out the 1.6 release.

00:17:14.640 --> 00:17:19.319
We will have burndown meetings--
these are again cross-company,

00:17:19.319 --> 00:17:21.720
across the community.

00:17:21.720 --> 00:17:24.210
And we will discuss
every single issue

00:17:24.210 --> 00:17:26.790
in the burndown meeting
for that release.

00:17:26.790 --> 00:17:28.740
We don't generally
hold the release.

00:17:28.740 --> 00:17:31.410
We have, as I mentioned,
a three-month cadence.

00:17:31.410 --> 00:17:35.280
And having that process
actually takes a lot of stress

00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:38.850
out of the whole process--

00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:41.272
having that release
cadence of three months.

00:17:41.272 --> 00:17:42.480
So we don't hold the release.

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:44.280
If an issue doesn't
make it this week,

00:17:44.280 --> 00:17:47.694
then it will make it
in the next 12 weeks.

00:17:47.694 --> 00:17:49.110
The only thing
that we hold it for

00:17:49.110 --> 00:17:51.664
is if there is a quality issue.

00:17:51.664 --> 00:17:53.830
And then the last piece
here is the roadmap process.

00:17:53.830 --> 00:17:55.829
And the community meetings
and burndown meetings

00:17:55.829 --> 00:17:58.690
are great, but how do
you get a view of what

00:17:58.690 --> 00:18:02.690
the project is trying to
achieve on a longer range?

00:18:02.690 --> 00:18:04.930
Roadmap process is
also a work in progress

00:18:04.930 --> 00:18:07.450
and is something that I've
been responsible for, along

00:18:07.450 --> 00:18:09.580
with others in the community.

00:18:09.580 --> 00:18:12.490
The heart of this
process is what we have

00:18:12.490 --> 00:18:13.910
called the features repository.

00:18:13.910 --> 00:18:16.210
It's a repository
where it essentially

00:18:16.210 --> 00:18:20.050
serves as our publicly open
backlog for the project.

00:18:20.050 --> 00:18:22.510
Again, anyone here can
go to the features repo

00:18:22.510 --> 00:18:24.520
and you can see what
are the features that

00:18:24.520 --> 00:18:27.100
are coming in the next release.

00:18:27.100 --> 00:18:28.980
And you can see their
status as they evolve.

00:18:28.980 --> 00:18:31.320
Obviously not everything
makes it and things

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:33.030
change during the
course of the release,

00:18:33.030 --> 00:18:35.430
but it's extremely open.

00:18:35.430 --> 00:18:37.140
You can go there
and you can see it.

00:18:37.140 --> 00:18:40.926
And that provides visibility, of
course, internally to the team

00:18:40.926 --> 00:18:42.300
as well-- to the
different SIGs--

00:18:42.300 --> 00:18:44.700
because they can see what
each SIG is working on

00:18:44.700 --> 00:18:46.980
and how that impacts.

00:18:46.980 --> 00:18:48.840
We have a feature freeze.

00:18:48.840 --> 00:18:51.120
So what can go into the
repo-- it shouldn't always

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:53.280
be evolving, obviously,
because there

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:54.690
has to be some predictability.

00:18:54.690 --> 00:18:57.390
So two weeks into
the 12-week release,

00:18:57.390 --> 00:18:58.950
we freeze the features repo.

00:18:58.950 --> 00:19:02.670
Anyone can go ahead and make
a recommendation of what

00:19:02.670 --> 00:19:04.110
should be in the features repo.

00:19:04.110 --> 00:19:06.450
They have to go to
their respective SIG

00:19:06.450 --> 00:19:09.990
and make sure that that feature
has a design proposal that

00:19:09.990 --> 00:19:13.110
has been reviewed by the
lead engineers in the SIG,

00:19:13.110 --> 00:19:16.800
and the SIG overall has
agreed to work on it.

00:19:16.800 --> 00:19:19.485
But then two weeks into the
release, that repo is frozen.

00:19:22.640 --> 00:19:25.160
The repo then also
drives the release notes

00:19:25.160 --> 00:19:27.470
and the documentation
for the release.

00:19:27.470 --> 00:19:29.840
Often in open source projects,
and certainly in ours,

00:19:29.840 --> 00:19:32.540
documentation has been
a major challenge--

00:19:32.540 --> 00:19:35.540
getting the docs to reflect
what has actually been developed

00:19:35.540 --> 00:19:38.570
in a way that is
consumable by users given

00:19:38.570 --> 00:19:41.720
the breadth of the
project and the intensity

00:19:41.720 --> 00:19:44.540
of the technical teams working
on it-- can be difficult.

00:19:44.540 --> 00:19:48.320
But having a central repository
allows us to at least plan

00:19:48.320 --> 00:19:54.470
for that, and also gate features
if they aren't well documented.

00:19:54.470 --> 00:20:00.600
Lastly, we've just started doing
annual and semiannual planning.

00:20:00.600 --> 00:20:02.690
This is where we get
the community together--

00:20:02.690 --> 00:20:04.430
all the different SIGs.

00:20:04.430 --> 00:20:06.950
We get them together
in an unconference

00:20:06.950 --> 00:20:09.830
to talk about the
state of the project

00:20:09.830 --> 00:20:12.420
and what we want to do
with it in the coming year.

00:20:12.420 --> 00:20:16.790
So we had a meeting in
December to talk about 2017

00:20:16.790 --> 00:20:20.150
and what Kubernetes
should aim for in 2017.

00:20:20.150 --> 00:20:24.350
And there were three top-level
things that came out of that.

00:20:24.350 --> 00:20:27.080
One is that we have to
work to scale the project,

00:20:27.080 --> 00:20:31.760
and so making
contribution easier,

00:20:31.760 --> 00:20:33.560
making changes to
improve our velocity

00:20:33.560 --> 00:20:34.970
to scale the
project-- that's one

00:20:34.970 --> 00:20:38.510
of our top priorities
as an example.

00:20:38.510 --> 00:20:43.430
Number two, we want to make it
easier for people to customize

00:20:43.430 --> 00:20:49.520
and add objects to our API
without having to restart,

00:20:49.520 --> 00:20:51.380
reboot Kubernetes.

00:20:51.380 --> 00:20:55.360
So that's another
thing in API Federation

00:20:55.360 --> 00:20:57.830
and associated features
is another track

00:20:57.830 --> 00:21:00.650
for the whole of 2017.

00:21:00.650 --> 00:21:04.820
And then the third,
related to extensibility,

00:21:04.820 --> 00:21:07.050
is ensuring conformance.

00:21:07.050 --> 00:21:09.440
Kubernetes has
become quite popular.

00:21:09.440 --> 00:21:10.670
There are many distributions.

00:21:10.670 --> 00:21:12.260
I think Brian recently counted.

00:21:12.260 --> 00:21:15.860
There are 30 or
so distributions.

00:21:15.860 --> 00:21:17.900
What is important to
users and one of the value

00:21:17.900 --> 00:21:19.610
propositions of
Kubernetes is that it

00:21:19.610 --> 00:21:23.480
is interoperable and portable
between these environments.

00:21:23.480 --> 00:21:25.880
In order to ensure
that interoperability,

00:21:25.880 --> 00:21:28.490
we're working on a set
of conformance tests

00:21:28.490 --> 00:21:29.990
so that each of
these distributions

00:21:29.990 --> 00:21:34.720
is conformant at
least at a base layer.

00:21:34.720 --> 00:21:37.000
So those are our
three themes for 2017.

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:40.600
There are others, but having
this type of unconference

00:21:40.600 --> 00:21:43.990
on a semiannual basis allows
us to get together and plan.

00:21:47.070 --> 00:21:50.070
So transparency, inclusion,
and some of these processes

00:21:50.070 --> 00:21:53.700
are good, and they're
working for us.

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:56.250
But they would not mean
anything if we didn't have

00:21:56.250 --> 00:21:58.710
the principle of ownership.

00:21:58.710 --> 00:22:02.400
Ownership in Kubernetes has
a very specific meaning,

00:22:02.400 --> 00:22:04.770
which is, first of all,
we encourage everyone

00:22:04.770 --> 00:22:08.156
in the community to take
responsibility and own a role.

00:22:08.156 --> 00:22:09.030
There are many roles.

00:22:09.030 --> 00:22:10.820
You can see some of them here.

00:22:10.820 --> 00:22:13.530
You can be a contributor,
you can lead a SIG,

00:22:13.530 --> 00:22:16.470
you can be part of the
product management group.

00:22:16.470 --> 00:22:18.720
There are many roles
in the release process

00:22:18.720 --> 00:22:19.470
that you can take.

00:22:19.470 --> 00:22:22.380
So there are engineering
and non-engineering roles

00:22:22.380 --> 00:22:24.120
in the project.

00:22:24.120 --> 00:22:27.540
But what ownership means is
not just that we encourage

00:22:27.540 --> 00:22:31.940
people to take these roles, but
that we also respect that role.

00:22:31.940 --> 00:22:35.720
And so people have a
chance to take on roles

00:22:35.720 --> 00:22:39.020
that they don't formally have.

00:22:39.020 --> 00:22:40.610
They haven't necessarily tried.

00:22:40.610 --> 00:22:43.880
We have engineers that do
product management and product

00:22:43.880 --> 00:22:45.710
managers that do
release management

00:22:45.710 --> 00:22:48.290
and so on and so forth.

00:22:48.290 --> 00:22:52.850
Respect for the owner means
that others in the community

00:22:52.850 --> 00:22:56.270
will step back and allow the
person that is taking that role

00:22:56.270 --> 00:23:00.030
and owns that role
to really define

00:23:00.030 --> 00:23:05.960
the community's answer for
that particular responsibility.

00:23:05.960 --> 00:23:07.760
And that allows
us to grow people

00:23:07.760 --> 00:23:11.450
into these different areas.

00:23:11.450 --> 00:23:15.470
Speaking of growth, one of
our challenges and something

00:23:15.470 --> 00:23:17.930
that we're working actively
on is growing people

00:23:17.930 --> 00:23:19.940
through the contributor ladder.

00:23:19.940 --> 00:23:23.000
We want to have a lot
more of our users--

00:23:23.000 --> 00:23:25.760
and many of our users actually
want to become contributors.

00:23:25.760 --> 00:23:28.980
We want to make it easy for
them to join as contributors

00:23:28.980 --> 00:23:33.410
and then move up the
ladder as they become

00:23:33.410 --> 00:23:37.190
more facile and more well-known
in the Kubernetes community

00:23:37.190 --> 00:23:40.100
to become reviewers,
approvers, and ultimately

00:23:40.100 --> 00:23:45.910
a very select group of folks
that are in the owners file.

00:23:45.910 --> 00:23:48.250
This is the contribution path.

00:23:48.250 --> 00:23:51.340
People in the
community are rewarded

00:23:51.340 --> 00:23:53.890
for their ownership and
the responsibilities

00:23:53.890 --> 00:23:57.370
that they take, often in
the form of homemade awards

00:23:57.370 --> 00:24:02.562
but also blog mentions
and PR mentions.

00:24:02.562 --> 00:24:04.270
The release team is
often a critical one,

00:24:04.270 --> 00:24:07.780
and they are ones that
get a lot of gratitude

00:24:07.780 --> 00:24:11.220
from the community.

00:24:11.220 --> 00:24:14.280
Also Kubernetes has
become well enough known

00:24:14.280 --> 00:24:16.715
that people who
take on these roles

00:24:16.715 --> 00:24:18.090
and move up through
these ladders

00:24:18.090 --> 00:24:19.410
put that on their resume.

00:24:19.410 --> 00:24:22.650
And that is a qualification
that follows them

00:24:22.650 --> 00:24:25.380
from company to company as
they go on in their career.

00:24:29.260 --> 00:24:31.720
As we mentioned, we
don't know everything,

00:24:31.720 --> 00:24:35.200
and we especially don't
know of the challenges that

00:24:35.200 --> 00:24:38.920
will come as we scale
this project further.

00:24:38.920 --> 00:24:41.410
So we have several
learning mechanisms.

00:24:41.410 --> 00:24:44.440
We do retrospectives
after every release

00:24:44.440 --> 00:24:47.830
to talk about what went well
and what didn't go well.

00:24:47.830 --> 00:24:51.160
These retrospectives are
unique in that it's not

00:24:51.160 --> 00:24:52.630
a small group of--

00:24:52.630 --> 00:24:54.520
within my company, the
people that I know.

00:24:54.520 --> 00:24:58.780
But this is an open public
retrospective with people

00:24:58.780 --> 00:25:00.440
from many companies.

00:25:00.440 --> 00:25:02.530
And so it's very common
in Kubernetes ideas

00:25:02.530 --> 00:25:06.010
to get feedback from
someone over a chat

00:25:06.010 --> 00:25:10.550
or over the Slack channel and to
take that very, very seriously,

00:25:10.550 --> 00:25:14.447
and for that type of feedback
to actually be career enhancing.

00:25:18.650 --> 00:25:22.550
So that was about
our community and how

00:25:22.550 --> 00:25:24.320
it functions and the processes.

00:25:24.320 --> 00:25:27.140
This is a bit of user feedback.

00:25:27.140 --> 00:25:28.910
And what we found
is that the users,

00:25:28.910 --> 00:25:30.600
while they like the technology--

00:25:30.600 --> 00:25:34.610
Kubernetes has got
a lot of heritage

00:25:34.610 --> 00:25:36.800
from the different
companies that

00:25:36.800 --> 00:25:40.980
have been contributing to it in
terms of the technology itself.

00:25:40.980 --> 00:25:44.690
But really, I think the users
have been telling us that they

00:25:44.690 --> 00:25:45.839
value the transparency.

00:25:45.839 --> 00:25:48.380
They value the what you see is
what you get and the openness,

00:25:48.380 --> 00:25:52.210
and they value the community.

00:25:52.210 --> 00:25:54.700
They like the fact that
they can contribute

00:25:54.700 --> 00:25:58.630
and they can
influence, and they can

00:25:58.630 --> 00:26:01.300
work with all the different
companies that are involved.

00:26:04.310 --> 00:26:05.570
We recently did a survey.

00:26:05.570 --> 00:26:07.361
Actually, this survey
is still in progress.

00:26:07.361 --> 00:26:09.800
This is some early
feedback where

00:26:09.800 --> 00:26:13.880
I wanted to understand--
so this open source

00:26:13.880 --> 00:26:17.630
software-- how well accepted
and how important it

00:26:17.630 --> 00:26:21.830
is to companies that are large
in size-- enterprise companies.

00:26:21.830 --> 00:26:26.570
And as you can see here,
more than 50% of enterprises

00:26:26.570 --> 00:26:28.850
say that open source
software is either

00:26:28.850 --> 00:26:32.030
very important or moderately
important to them.

00:26:32.030 --> 00:26:34.394
So why is that?

00:26:34.394 --> 00:26:35.810
And these are some
of the benefits

00:26:35.810 --> 00:26:39.390
that they say are important.

00:26:39.390 --> 00:26:41.630
Number one, the ability
to customize it.

00:26:41.630 --> 00:26:44.240
And that's particularly
true with Kubernetes

00:26:44.240 --> 00:26:47.510
because if you want to
install it on premise,

00:26:47.510 --> 00:26:49.800
you can add and modify--

00:26:49.800 --> 00:26:55.160
adjust almost all
of the components--

00:26:55.160 --> 00:26:58.190
the scheduler, the
monitoring solution,

00:26:58.190 --> 00:26:59.300
the networking solution.

00:26:59.300 --> 00:27:02.240
Pretty much all parts
of it are adaptable

00:27:02.240 --> 00:27:04.399
and can be changed and adjusted.

00:27:04.399 --> 00:27:06.440
The other one, which I
think is very interesting,

00:27:06.440 --> 00:27:08.300
is the lower barrier to entry.

00:27:08.300 --> 00:27:13.910
Obviously, it's free, and
many of our ultimate customers

00:27:13.910 --> 00:27:15.500
start out trying it.

00:27:15.500 --> 00:27:19.910
And so by the time they actually
use a commercial version,

00:27:19.910 --> 00:27:22.400
and any of them-- any of
the commercial versions--

00:27:22.400 --> 00:27:23.630
they already have experience.

00:27:23.630 --> 00:27:25.046
They have tried
it out and they've

00:27:25.046 --> 00:27:27.980
decided that this is really
what they want to do.

00:27:27.980 --> 00:27:31.092
And they've become sometimes
contributors and members

00:27:31.092 --> 00:27:33.050
of the community already,
which makes it easier

00:27:33.050 --> 00:27:34.670
to work together.

00:27:34.670 --> 00:27:42.020
And then I think the last two,
or the second two after this--

00:27:42.020 --> 00:27:46.970
faster rate of innovation and
the elimination of lock-in

00:27:46.970 --> 00:27:49.550
that comes with
proprietary software.

00:27:49.550 --> 00:27:51.530
Those are also
extremely important.

00:27:51.530 --> 00:27:53.390
I think the faster
rate of innovation

00:27:53.390 --> 00:27:55.610
is something that if
you join our community

00:27:55.610 --> 00:27:57.526
or if you're already
members of the community,

00:27:57.526 --> 00:27:58.310
you will see that.

00:27:58.310 --> 00:28:01.070
It's actually, I would
say arguably faster

00:28:01.070 --> 00:28:04.400
than the innovation that takes
place inside any of the member

00:28:04.400 --> 00:28:05.372
companies.

00:28:07.970 --> 00:28:10.130
As example of user
contributions,

00:28:10.130 --> 00:28:12.369
this is just a sampling
of some of the user talks

00:28:12.369 --> 00:28:13.160
that are coming up.

00:28:13.160 --> 00:28:16.430
So we have our annual
conference for Kubernetes--

00:28:16.430 --> 00:28:18.530
biannual conference
for Kuberentes--

00:28:18.530 --> 00:28:22.220
coming up in two
weeks in Berlin.

00:28:22.220 --> 00:28:25.220
And these are a sampling
of some of the user talks.

00:28:25.220 --> 00:28:27.260
One of the things that
users tell us they

00:28:27.260 --> 00:28:31.970
enjoy most is not talking to
Google or a CoreOS or Red Hat

00:28:31.970 --> 00:28:35.270
or any of the vendors,
but talking to other users

00:28:35.270 --> 00:28:37.490
about how did they
do the deployment.

00:28:37.490 --> 00:28:41.270
And this is some of the
currently trending areas

00:28:41.270 --> 00:28:42.300
of interest.

00:28:42.300 --> 00:28:47.780
So how do you deploy Kubernetes
in a private cloud OnPrem

00:28:47.780 --> 00:28:52.880
environment with
Legacy load balancers?

00:28:52.880 --> 00:28:56.990
How do you use PostgreSQL
in a clustered HA set up

00:28:56.990 --> 00:28:58.770
with Kubernetes?

00:28:58.770 --> 00:29:01.190
And Zalando will be
talking about how they're

00:29:01.190 --> 00:29:04.940
doing that using stateful sets.

00:29:04.940 --> 00:29:09.470
Our users are using TensorFlow
on top of Kubernetes

00:29:09.470 --> 00:29:12.860
with GPUs on different
types of clouds.

00:29:12.860 --> 00:29:15.200
This is actually
information that's

00:29:15.200 --> 00:29:17.660
very helpful from a product
and planning perspective

00:29:17.660 --> 00:29:20.780
because I can see what the
users are interested in

00:29:20.780 --> 00:29:24.320
and we can focus our roadmap
on meeting their needs.

00:29:24.320 --> 00:29:25.320
And some of the others--

00:29:25.320 --> 00:29:27.900
CI/CD migration-- that's
a very common one.

00:29:27.900 --> 00:29:30.965
Many of our users have
reference architectures

00:29:30.965 --> 00:29:32.340
that they have
developed for that

00:29:32.340 --> 00:29:33.850
and they share with each other.

00:29:33.850 --> 00:29:36.210
We also see a lot of
migration from a variety

00:29:36.210 --> 00:29:40.790
of different PaaSes
to Kubernetes.

00:29:40.790 --> 00:29:44.850
So that was a little
bit about our users.

00:29:44.850 --> 00:29:47.840
And I mentioned that as
a Google product manager,

00:29:47.840 --> 00:29:51.950
I have a view of what our
users are doing on Google.

00:29:51.950 --> 00:29:54.710
But by being a member
of the community,

00:29:54.710 --> 00:29:58.160
I can get a much better
view of the whole elephant

00:29:58.160 --> 00:30:00.350
and what our users are
doing in other clouds

00:30:00.350 --> 00:30:04.154
and what they are
looking for on premise.

00:30:04.154 --> 00:30:05.570
That's where our
partner with some

00:30:05.570 --> 00:30:06.980
of the members of
our community--

00:30:06.980 --> 00:30:10.160
and I'm going to invite
Brandon Philips, who

00:30:10.160 --> 00:30:11.620
is the CTO of CoreOS.

00:30:11.620 --> 00:30:14.450
CoreOS has been one
of the first companies

00:30:14.450 --> 00:30:16.430
to join the
Kubernetes community,

00:30:16.430 --> 00:30:20.390
and they bring a perspective
from both on premise and multi

00:30:20.390 --> 00:30:24.360
cloud and hybrid users
that I'd like you to hear.

00:30:28.264 --> 00:30:30.774
[APPLAUSE]

00:30:30.774 --> 00:30:31.690
BRANDON PHILIPS: Cool.

00:30:31.690 --> 00:30:33.170
Thank you for the introduction.

00:30:33.170 --> 00:30:37.870
So what I wanted to talk about
was our involvement inside

00:30:37.870 --> 00:30:40.325
of the Kubernetes project
and some of the customers

00:30:40.325 --> 00:30:41.950
that we have been
working with and what

00:30:41.950 --> 00:30:43.510
we've been seeing
in the community,

00:30:43.510 --> 00:30:47.080
and our strategy in interacting
with the Kubernetes community

00:30:47.080 --> 00:30:48.010
overall.

00:30:48.010 --> 00:30:55.480
So just for a little background,
CoreOS started in 2013.

00:30:55.480 --> 00:30:57.850
Ironically, back
then we had thought

00:30:57.850 --> 00:31:00.230
that one of the biggest risks
that we would have starting

00:31:00.230 --> 00:31:02.890
the company was that the market
wouldn't understand containers.

00:31:02.890 --> 00:31:04.570
I think that risk
has been mitigated

00:31:04.570 --> 00:31:07.790
with all the excitement
over the last few years.

00:31:07.790 --> 00:31:10.220
But what we're really
trying to do is two things.

00:31:10.220 --> 00:31:12.850
One is that we're trying to--

00:31:12.850 --> 00:31:15.160
as a big overarching theme--

00:31:15.160 --> 00:31:17.590
we like to say that we're
trying to secure the internet.

00:31:17.590 --> 00:31:21.520
And that's the long-term
mission of the company.

00:31:21.520 --> 00:31:23.410
Now, the tactic that
we take is that we

00:31:23.410 --> 00:31:24.970
try to take best
practices that have

00:31:24.970 --> 00:31:26.830
been seen in a lot of
these internet giants.

00:31:26.830 --> 00:31:29.620
What we used to call GIFEE,
or Google's Infrastructure

00:31:29.620 --> 00:31:30.850
for Everybody Else--

00:31:30.850 --> 00:31:32.950
and bring these
technologies and help

00:31:32.950 --> 00:31:35.870
companies and organizations
be successful with them.

00:31:35.870 --> 00:31:37.870
So that's what we do at CoreOS.

00:31:37.870 --> 00:31:42.520
And so we started our journey
particularly around Kubernetes,

00:31:42.520 --> 00:31:44.410
before even Kubernetes
came out, we

00:31:44.410 --> 00:31:45.910
had a project that
we had introduced

00:31:45.910 --> 00:31:47.840
called Fleet into the market.

00:31:47.840 --> 00:31:51.199
We started this in late
2013, and we were trying

00:31:51.199 --> 00:31:52.240
to fill this white space.

00:31:52.240 --> 00:31:55.280
Containers are great, but they
were stuck on single hosts.

00:31:55.280 --> 00:31:58.000
And so we built this
clustering system,

00:31:58.000 --> 00:32:01.000
and it was built on a bunch
of different technologies used

00:32:01.000 --> 00:32:04.180
by a bunch of different
organizations.

00:32:04.180 --> 00:32:06.780
And it was going pretty well,
and then unbeknownst to us,

00:32:06.780 --> 00:32:10.450
there was a great set of
people inside of Google

00:32:10.450 --> 00:32:12.400
working on Kubernetes.

00:32:12.400 --> 00:32:16.860
And so this project was
introduced a few months--

00:32:16.860 --> 00:32:19.470
about six to eight months
after we started Fleet.

00:32:19.470 --> 00:32:22.047
And so pretty rapidly
we saw that Kubernetes

00:32:22.047 --> 00:32:24.630
filled in a lot of the gaps that
we were planning to build out

00:32:24.630 --> 00:32:26.910
in Fleet, and so we
decided to join up

00:32:26.910 --> 00:32:28.350
with the Kubernetes community.

00:32:28.350 --> 00:32:30.308
And we've been deeply
involved pretty much ever

00:32:30.308 --> 00:32:33.490
since the beginning
of the project.

00:32:33.490 --> 00:32:35.110
And the reason that
I think Kubernetes

00:32:35.110 --> 00:32:37.870
got such a huge
start initially was

00:32:37.870 --> 00:32:40.810
that it was really informed by
a lot of Google's experience

00:32:40.810 --> 00:32:45.220
running these sorts of
systems in production.

00:32:45.220 --> 00:32:45.720
All right.

00:32:45.720 --> 00:32:49.350
So what is our journey
that we take with customers

00:32:49.350 --> 00:32:50.850
on a regular basis?

00:32:50.850 --> 00:32:54.240
Often times, customers are not
going to start-- particularly

00:32:54.240 --> 00:32:58.020
people who are just thinking
about that cloud transition,

00:32:58.020 --> 00:33:00.450
just learning and understanding
containers-- they're not

00:33:00.450 --> 00:33:03.450
going to start by just
biting off Kubernetes

00:33:03.450 --> 00:33:04.690
from the very beginning.

00:33:04.690 --> 00:33:08.460
So what we do is we help a
lot of different organizations

00:33:08.460 --> 00:33:10.800
think about what is
their cloud strategy,

00:33:10.800 --> 00:33:13.170
start thinking about containers
and how that influences

00:33:13.170 --> 00:33:16.680
and changes the way that
organization works together,

00:33:16.680 --> 00:33:19.650
bring these things together
in different environments

00:33:19.650 --> 00:33:21.400
with some of our products--

00:33:21.400 --> 00:33:24.540
and then moving them through the
transition towards Kubernetes

00:33:24.540 --> 00:33:27.840
and the Kubernetes product
that we have called Tectonic.

00:33:27.840 --> 00:33:31.266
So it really is not
just a technology choice

00:33:31.266 --> 00:33:32.640
for a lot of these
organizations,

00:33:32.640 --> 00:33:35.130
but it's also a choice
of making changes

00:33:35.130 --> 00:33:39.890
about how they approach
software development,

00:33:39.890 --> 00:33:44.660
and also how they approach
rolling out and managing

00:33:44.660 --> 00:33:47.380
this infrastructure.

00:33:47.380 --> 00:33:50.980
So in particular, what we
do with our products is

00:33:50.980 --> 00:33:53.560
we like to say that they are
self-driving, which really

00:33:53.560 --> 00:33:56.680
is just this continuous
process of delivering

00:33:56.680 --> 00:33:59.130
the latest in open
source technology

00:33:59.130 --> 00:34:01.510
into customers' environments,
getting feedback

00:34:01.510 --> 00:34:03.520
from those customers
on what's working well,

00:34:03.520 --> 00:34:06.730
what isn't working well, having
our engineering teams directly

00:34:06.730 --> 00:34:08.980
involved inside
of the communities

00:34:08.980 --> 00:34:12.790
to ensure that those projects
are representing what they

00:34:12.790 --> 00:34:16.000
actually need, and then
continuing that cycle

00:34:16.000 --> 00:34:17.290
release after release.

00:34:17.290 --> 00:34:19.150
What we like to say
is that we deliver

00:34:19.150 --> 00:34:21.199
pure upstream Kubernetes
for our customers,

00:34:21.199 --> 00:34:22.989
but we do it in a
particular way that

00:34:22.989 --> 00:34:26.020
enables them to actually digest
and get ahold of that value.

00:34:29.909 --> 00:34:34.550
So in order to have this
cycle where we aren't waiting

00:34:34.550 --> 00:34:37.429
for months or years before
delivering an open source

00:34:37.429 --> 00:34:39.409
project to an
enterprise, we obviously

00:34:39.409 --> 00:34:41.870
need to be deeply involved
in the release process

00:34:41.870 --> 00:34:44.480
to ensure that the open
source projects are delivering

00:34:44.480 --> 00:34:47.090
something that can be
used right of the shelf

00:34:47.090 --> 00:34:50.330
without modifications by
these large organizations.

00:34:50.330 --> 00:34:53.690
And so one of the things
that has evolved inside

00:34:53.690 --> 00:34:55.880
of the Kubernetes is our
involvement in the release

00:34:55.880 --> 00:34:58.220
process, but also a
really rigorous release

00:34:58.220 --> 00:35:02.630
process that is adopted
by the entire community.

00:35:02.630 --> 00:35:05.720
So one of the really unique
things about Kubernetes

00:35:05.720 --> 00:35:08.570
as an open source
project is it's

00:35:08.570 --> 00:35:15.350
taken a very practiced
and pragmatic approach

00:35:15.350 --> 00:35:16.850
to release management.

00:35:16.850 --> 00:35:19.910
So inside of Kubernetes we
essentially, at any given time,

00:35:19.910 --> 00:35:22.190
have three releases going on.

00:35:22.190 --> 00:35:23.660
We have the latest release--

00:35:23.660 --> 00:35:25.670
the latest minor
release is what we call

00:35:25.670 --> 00:35:27.560
it, which right now is 1.5.

00:35:27.560 --> 00:35:29.870
We have then two
releases that we continue

00:35:29.870 --> 00:35:33.050
to maintain with security
fixes and bug fixes,

00:35:33.050 --> 00:35:37.070
and these are available to
users of our product Tectonic,

00:35:37.070 --> 00:35:40.270
available to users of
Google's GKE, et cetera.

00:35:40.270 --> 00:35:42.080
But what this means
is that customers

00:35:42.080 --> 00:35:45.380
are given nine to 12
months of time period

00:35:45.380 --> 00:35:48.830
from when they adopt
something to it continuing

00:35:48.830 --> 00:35:53.360
to get security updates to
it essentially to eventually

00:35:53.360 --> 00:35:54.320
becoming end of life.

00:35:54.320 --> 00:35:56.840
So they have a very
long confidence window

00:35:56.840 --> 00:36:00.280
of a particular
release being something

00:36:00.280 --> 00:36:02.820
that they can rely on for
a long period of time.

00:36:02.820 --> 00:36:05.719
And then obviously, throughout
that entire process,

00:36:05.719 --> 00:36:07.260
these are minor
release bumps, and we

00:36:07.260 --> 00:36:08.850
ensure that the
upgrade paths are well

00:36:08.850 --> 00:36:11.610
tested and well understood.

00:36:11.610 --> 00:36:14.790
So to give you a sense of
what these minor releases look

00:36:14.790 --> 00:36:15.570
like--

00:36:15.570 --> 00:36:17.130
Aparna touched on
this a little bit--

00:36:17.130 --> 00:36:20.380
we are currently in the
middle of a release cycle.

00:36:20.380 --> 00:36:22.920
Kubernetes 1.6 is
the next release

00:36:22.920 --> 00:36:24.310
that will be coming out.

00:36:24.310 --> 00:36:27.780
And so we essentially run
through this calendar,

00:36:27.780 --> 00:36:30.450
where we spend about
four or six weeks coding

00:36:30.450 --> 00:36:32.910
depending on the release.

00:36:32.910 --> 00:36:34.410
We go through a
number of alphas,

00:36:34.410 --> 00:36:37.890
we go through and do a
beta, we start code freezes,

00:36:37.890 --> 00:36:39.730
and then we finally
release the project.

00:36:39.730 --> 00:36:42.980
And we this about every quarter,
every three months or so.

00:36:42.980 --> 00:36:45.540
And meanwhile, as security
vulnerabilities come out,

00:36:45.540 --> 00:36:49.560
as bug fixes are needed, we
are making these back port

00:36:49.560 --> 00:36:56.440
and cherry pick releases into
the 1.5, 1.4, and 1.3 branches.

00:36:56.440 --> 00:36:59.420
So one of the things
about open source software

00:36:59.420 --> 00:37:01.310
and good software
practices in general

00:37:01.310 --> 00:37:04.010
is that it's not an
individual member that

00:37:04.010 --> 00:37:05.520
makes it really successful.

00:37:05.520 --> 00:37:08.380
It takes a big team, and
inside of Kubernetes,

00:37:08.380 --> 00:37:10.190
there's a lot of
people involved--

00:37:10.190 --> 00:37:13.220
from product managers to
documentation people to people

00:37:13.220 --> 00:37:18.200
doing triage of incoming bugs,
and then at the very top is

00:37:18.200 --> 00:37:23.100
a person who is overall
responsible for the release--

00:37:23.100 --> 00:37:26.430
the release manager.

00:37:26.430 --> 00:37:29.220
And their goal is
to one, make sure

00:37:29.220 --> 00:37:33.420
the entire team is filled in,
and then two, that the release

00:37:33.420 --> 00:37:35.940
actually happens on time.

00:37:35.940 --> 00:37:37.440
So one of the things
that's happened

00:37:37.440 --> 00:37:39.450
in this release--
the 1.6 release

00:37:39.450 --> 00:37:42.840
that I'm really happy about-- is
that CoreOS has gotten involved

00:37:42.840 --> 00:37:46.140
and one of our
engineers, Dan Gillespie,

00:37:46.140 --> 00:37:49.560
is the release manager for
this release in 1.6, which

00:37:49.560 --> 00:37:51.810
is pretty cool because
this is the continuing

00:37:51.810 --> 00:37:55.830
effort of the community to
make all the responsibilities

00:37:55.830 --> 00:38:01.130
of the project across
different organizations that

00:38:01.130 --> 00:38:01.800
are involved.

00:38:01.800 --> 00:38:03.650
So this will be
the first release

00:38:03.650 --> 00:38:05.902
where the release manager
isn't a Google employee,

00:38:05.902 --> 00:38:08.360
and I think this is an important
milestone for the project.

00:38:11.110 --> 00:38:14.910
So another piece of
releasing is testing.

00:38:14.910 --> 00:38:16.770
Now, we have this
diagram of Kubernetes

00:38:16.770 --> 00:38:18.240
that we used earlier.

00:38:18.240 --> 00:38:20.240
There's a lot of moving
parts inside Kubernetes,

00:38:20.240 --> 00:38:22.470
as this diagram would
lead you to believe.

00:38:22.470 --> 00:38:25.860
And what we do is a really,
really sophisticated set

00:38:25.860 --> 00:38:26.377
of testing.

00:38:26.377 --> 00:38:28.710
If you haven't taken a look
at the Kubernetes dashboards

00:38:28.710 --> 00:38:31.050
at any point, a lot
of it allows you

00:38:31.050 --> 00:38:33.360
to drill down across a
lot of different metrics,

00:38:33.360 --> 00:38:35.100
across different
cloud providers,

00:38:35.100 --> 00:38:38.400
across different deployment
types, et cetera,

00:38:38.400 --> 00:38:40.980
and drill down and see is
the code actually healthy?

00:38:40.980 --> 00:38:42.830
Is it operating as it should?

00:38:42.830 --> 00:38:45.870
One of my favorite dashboards
that Kubernetes has

00:38:45.870 --> 00:38:47.809
is of individual
nodes where we're

00:38:47.809 --> 00:38:50.350
able to drill down and see how
much memory is being consumed,

00:38:50.350 --> 00:38:53.300
how much CPU is being
consumed across the test.

00:38:56.030 --> 00:38:59.300
Another big change that we've
had in the last few months

00:38:59.300 --> 00:39:03.440
is that we've made the security
release process something that

00:39:03.440 --> 00:39:06.590
is run by the community.

00:39:06.590 --> 00:39:09.890
So security releases are pretty
important to any open source

00:39:09.890 --> 00:39:12.950
software, and it's really
easy to make these things

00:39:12.950 --> 00:39:15.530
sort of an ad hoc process.

00:39:15.530 --> 00:39:19.250
Security vulnerabilities come
out every couple of months

00:39:19.250 --> 00:39:21.920
no matter how good your
engineering practices are,

00:39:21.920 --> 00:39:24.380
and so for a lot of
projects, they just

00:39:24.380 --> 00:39:25.945
deal with them as they come.

00:39:25.945 --> 00:39:28.070
But one of the things that
Kubernetes community has

00:39:28.070 --> 00:39:30.740
done well is that we
identify a problem,

00:39:30.740 --> 00:39:33.200
we try to create a process
to fix that problem reliably

00:39:33.200 --> 00:39:36.620
time and time again, and
delegate responsibilities

00:39:36.620 --> 00:39:38.660
of getting that thing fixed.

00:39:38.660 --> 00:39:40.130
And so the security
release process

00:39:40.130 --> 00:39:43.220
allows the community to go
through, handle disclosures

00:39:43.220 --> 00:39:45.920
quickly, figure out
a communication plan

00:39:45.920 --> 00:39:47.930
with the users,
communicate that plan

00:39:47.930 --> 00:39:50.420
and actually give
them actionable data.

00:39:50.420 --> 00:39:53.120
We'll be running
through this process

00:39:53.120 --> 00:39:55.070
every time that somebody
discloses an issue,

00:39:55.070 --> 00:39:57.710
whether it ends up being an
actual security-impacting issue

00:39:57.710 --> 00:39:58.550
or not.

00:39:58.550 --> 00:40:01.820
And at the end, we try to
make a retrospective of, hey,

00:40:01.820 --> 00:40:03.850
did this security
process actually work?

00:40:03.850 --> 00:40:05.660
What can we do better next time?

00:40:05.660 --> 00:40:06.890
Et cetera.

00:40:06.890 --> 00:40:11.840
This is a quote in the SRE--
the Site Reliability Engineering

00:40:11.840 --> 00:40:13.050
book--

00:40:13.050 --> 00:40:15.800
there's this idea of
blameless retrospectives.

00:40:15.800 --> 00:40:18.050
And this is one
that I really like--

00:40:18.050 --> 00:40:19.990
the cost of failure
is education.

00:40:19.990 --> 00:40:22.880
And this is really about, if
our security process doesn't

00:40:22.880 --> 00:40:25.580
go well or our release
process doesn't go well,

00:40:25.580 --> 00:40:29.300
all we can do is reflect on what
didn't go well, get together

00:40:29.300 --> 00:40:32.311
as a team, and actually improve
the process for the next time

00:40:32.311 --> 00:40:32.810
around.

00:40:35.730 --> 00:40:36.680
Cool.

00:40:36.680 --> 00:40:40.430
So a big reason that I think
Kubernetes has gotten extremely

00:40:40.430 --> 00:40:43.100
popular, and one of the things
that we're directly involved

00:40:43.100 --> 00:40:46.190
in, is making
Kubernetes successful

00:40:46.190 --> 00:40:48.920
wherever people have
compute network in storage.

00:40:48.920 --> 00:40:50.720
And we've done this
in a variety of ways.

00:40:57.010 --> 00:41:00.710
The dream for people
of cloud portability

00:41:00.710 --> 00:41:03.971
is that you're able to build
an application for the cloud

00:41:03.971 --> 00:41:06.470
and then you're able to run it
in your existing data center.

00:41:06.470 --> 00:41:08.960
For a lot of people, they
have a data center footprint

00:41:08.960 --> 00:41:10.200
of some sort.

00:41:10.200 --> 00:41:13.220
You're able to run it on
your individual developers'

00:41:13.220 --> 00:41:15.620
machines, so they are able
to have a local dev test

00:41:15.620 --> 00:41:17.060
environment.

00:41:17.060 --> 00:41:20.769
You're able to deploy it into
a public cloud environment,

00:41:20.769 --> 00:41:22.810
maybe run it on a second
cloud environment, which

00:41:22.810 --> 00:41:27.260
we're finding is a very common
pattern for a lot of people--

00:41:27.260 --> 00:41:29.960
and maybe a third
cloud environment.

00:41:29.960 --> 00:41:33.730
And so as an example of this, we
have a customer, Ticketmaster,

00:41:33.730 --> 00:41:35.114
that we've worked with.

00:41:35.114 --> 00:41:37.530
I was surprised when we started
working with Ticketmaster.

00:41:37.530 --> 00:41:40.600
I didn't realized they are a
40-year-old technology company.

00:41:40.600 --> 00:41:42.290
But as a 40-year-old
technology company,

00:41:42.290 --> 00:41:44.230
they have a lot of challenges.

00:41:44.230 --> 00:41:46.600
They have a lot of
virtual machine footprint.

00:41:46.600 --> 00:41:48.760
They have about 22,000
virtual machines

00:41:48.760 --> 00:41:51.580
that they manage on
their own data centers

00:41:51.580 --> 00:41:54.850
that they've purchased
all the hardware for.

00:41:54.850 --> 00:41:56.740
They have a lot of
different products.

00:41:56.740 --> 00:41:59.380
They have a lot of different
engineers, et cetera.

00:41:59.380 --> 00:42:01.540
But what they deal with
every day is what they call

00:42:01.540 --> 00:42:05.380
their self-inflicted DDOS
as a service, which is,

00:42:05.380 --> 00:42:07.360
when a new concert
ticket comes out--

00:42:07.360 --> 00:42:10.360
maybe Beyonce has
announced a new tour--

00:42:10.360 --> 00:42:13.600
they essentially have this
one moment in time where

00:42:13.600 --> 00:42:14.860
they get a ton of traffic.

00:42:14.860 --> 00:42:19.060
And obviously they would like
to move a lot of this traffic

00:42:19.060 --> 00:42:21.389
and a lot of this responsibility
onto a public cloud.

00:42:21.389 --> 00:42:22.930
And so with Kubernetes,
we're helping

00:42:22.930 --> 00:42:25.750
them to deploy Kubernetes
both in their data center

00:42:25.750 --> 00:42:28.580
and to move some of their
workload to the public cloud.

00:42:28.580 --> 00:42:31.030
And that's all enabled
because of the abstractions

00:42:31.030 --> 00:42:34.560
and because of the
technology of Kubernetes.

00:42:34.560 --> 00:42:37.920
And so in order
to get them there,

00:42:37.920 --> 00:42:40.890
in order to get a lot of these
companies there, what we do

00:42:40.890 --> 00:42:44.070
is that we fill in a lot of
the white spaces for where

00:42:44.070 --> 00:42:48.810
Kubernetes is not able to
adapt to any environment.

00:42:48.810 --> 00:42:51.420
So some of the things that
we've introduced over time

00:42:51.420 --> 00:42:54.150
are things like Dex, which
is an identity provider that

00:42:54.150 --> 00:42:57.180
allows people to plug in to
their existing Active Directory

00:42:57.180 --> 00:43:00.210
LDAP services, or other things
like Flannel, where we're

00:43:00.210 --> 00:43:04.560
able to bring the Kubernetes
opinion and model of networking

00:43:04.560 --> 00:43:07.830
across any public cloud
or into people's data

00:43:07.830 --> 00:43:12.030
centers with ease, without a
lot of work with their network

00:43:12.030 --> 00:43:13.590
operations team.

00:43:13.590 --> 00:43:16.290
And that goes across a lot of
what we do in the open source.

00:43:16.290 --> 00:43:17.910
We work closely with Kubernetes.

00:43:17.910 --> 00:43:20.130
We fill in white
spaces where Kubernetes

00:43:20.130 --> 00:43:22.320
is unable to go where
we need it to go.

00:43:22.320 --> 00:43:24.030
And we continue to
iterate and make sure

00:43:24.030 --> 00:43:27.000
that it is successful as
a multi cloud platform.

00:43:29.980 --> 00:43:33.980
So what Kubernetes is at the
end of the day is really,

00:43:33.980 --> 00:43:39.105
I think, a way of expressing a
consumption of compute network

00:43:39.105 --> 00:43:42.160
and storage that can
be used and abstracted

00:43:42.160 --> 00:43:46.000
across any sort
of infrastructure.

00:43:46.000 --> 00:43:47.960
And that means
that whether you're

00:43:47.960 --> 00:43:52.340
using Google Cloud or any of
the other public clouds, or even

00:43:52.340 --> 00:43:55.460
Bare Metal, Kubernetes
should reliably

00:43:55.460 --> 00:43:59.240
work in the exact same
way across those systems.

00:43:59.240 --> 00:44:02.210
And it's our responsibility,
as members of the Kubernetes

00:44:02.210 --> 00:44:04.370
communities, and
companies helping

00:44:04.370 --> 00:44:09.440
to make these users successful,
that we build the technology

00:44:09.440 --> 00:44:11.840
in such a way that
this is actually true

00:44:11.840 --> 00:44:15.910
and that we can bring
this idea to fruition.

00:44:15.910 --> 00:44:20.880
So I just wanted to end with a
quick recap about the journey

00:44:20.880 --> 00:44:23.220
that we've gone through
with Kubernetes and really

00:44:23.220 --> 00:44:25.410
what it's trying to tackle.

00:44:25.410 --> 00:44:27.120
Kubernetes is this
container-centric

00:44:27.120 --> 00:44:30.270
infrastructure and
software that's

00:44:30.270 --> 00:44:33.855
designed to help people use
some of the best practices that

00:44:33.855 --> 00:44:37.680
have evolved from Google and
other people over the years.

00:44:37.680 --> 00:44:41.040
It's a system that's designed
to run anywhere, whether you're

00:44:41.040 --> 00:44:44.630
in your cloud provider,
on your laptop,

00:44:44.630 --> 00:44:48.680
or in your Bare
Metal environments.

00:44:48.680 --> 00:44:51.100
It is a huge active community.

00:44:51.100 --> 00:44:53.050
We have people from
all over the globe,

00:44:53.050 --> 00:44:55.240
like Aparna said, participating.

00:44:55.240 --> 00:44:57.610
And those people aren't
just necessarily engineers.

00:44:57.610 --> 00:45:00.190
We have very talented product
managers and documentation

00:45:00.190 --> 00:45:05.160
experts, who are making
the project not just a fun

00:45:05.160 --> 00:45:08.116
open source science experiment,
but an interesting and useful

00:45:08.116 --> 00:45:10.240
product that people can
actually take off the shelf

00:45:10.240 --> 00:45:14.240
and use in their
infrastructure today.

00:45:14.240 --> 00:45:17.430
And with all these data
points that we've discussed,

00:45:17.430 --> 00:45:21.644
it's actively in use by
large organizations today.

00:45:21.644 --> 00:45:23.560
I think it's really easy
as software engineers

00:45:23.560 --> 00:45:25.870
to get excited about how
cool a technology, is

00:45:25.870 --> 00:45:27.324
but really the
value of technology

00:45:27.324 --> 00:45:28.990
doesn't come out until
you have somebody

00:45:28.990 --> 00:45:32.530
who is actively using it and
giving you that feedback.

00:45:32.530 --> 00:45:35.080
And I'm really happy
to say that Kubernetes

00:45:35.080 --> 00:45:37.510
is doing that on a
very regular basis

00:45:37.510 --> 00:45:42.110
with a growing number of
organizations using it.

00:45:42.110 --> 00:45:44.450
And of course, there's
a lot of fun as well.

00:45:44.450 --> 00:45:48.710
This community is a
super dynamic community.

00:45:48.710 --> 00:45:53.886
People are excited about the way
that we're developing software.

00:45:53.886 --> 00:45:55.760
They're excited about
working with each other

00:45:55.760 --> 00:45:59.550
across different organizations.

00:45:59.550 --> 00:46:02.840
And I think that is an
important part of building

00:46:02.840 --> 00:46:04.450
any sort of community.

00:46:04.450 --> 00:46:07.190
With a little bit of
branding and a good group

00:46:07.190 --> 00:46:09.320
of people and some
good processes,

00:46:09.320 --> 00:46:12.170
you can build something
awesome and we're

00:46:12.170 --> 00:46:15.300
doing that at Kubernetes

00:46:15.300 --> 00:46:20.460
So with this, I'd like to invite
you to join all of us working

00:46:20.460 --> 00:46:23.610
on Kubernetes at
github.com/kubernetes.

00:46:23.610 --> 00:46:28.500
There you can find the code,
but also importantly information

00:46:28.500 --> 00:46:31.440
on how to find us all--
so the mailing lists,

00:46:31.440 --> 00:46:34.320
the community Hangouts,
all the details that you

00:46:34.320 --> 00:46:35.920
might need to get involved.

00:46:35.920 --> 00:46:37.830
And if you ever
have any questions,

00:46:37.830 --> 00:46:41.190
there's a lot of friendly people
that will answer you either

00:46:41.190 --> 00:46:43.680
on our Slacker mailing list.

00:46:43.680 --> 00:46:46.670
No question will be
rejected, so please join us.

00:46:46.670 --> 00:46:49.210
And with that, I thank
you for your attention.

00:46:49.210 --> 00:46:50.410
Thanks.

00:46:50.410 --> 00:47:00.960
[MUSIC PLAYING]

